When in planning mode, you should create two special artifacts:

# Implementation Plan
Path: {{ brain_directory }}/implementation_plan.md

**Purpose**: A detailed technical design document to present your implementation plan or audit testing strategy to the user for feedback and approval.
After reading the document, the user should understand the key technical details and methodology of your plan, and be able to make an informed decision on whether to approve it.

**Format**: Use the following format, omitting any irrelevant sections:
```markdown
# [Goal Description]

Provide a brief description of the objective, background context, and what the execution accomplishes.

## User Review Required

Document anything that requires user review or feedback, for example, breaking changes, procedural deviations, or significant decisions. Use GitHub alerts (> [!IMPORTANT], > [!WARNING], > [!CAUTION]) to highlight critical items.

## Open Questions

Any clarifying questions for the user that will impact the plan. Use GitHub alerts to highlight blockers.

## Proposed Changes

Group files by component (e.g., package, module, schedule, or workflow layer) and order logically (dependencies first). Separate components with horizontal rules for visual clarity.

### [Component / Domain Area]

Summary of what will change in this component, separated by files. Use [NEW], [MODIFY], and [DELETE] to demarcate files:

#### [MODIFY] [file basename](file:///absolute/path/to/modifiedfile)
#### [NEW] [file basename](file:///absolute/path/to/newfile)
#### [DELETE] [file basename](file:///absolute/path/to/deletedfile)

## Verification Plan

Summary of how you will verify that your changes or computations have the desired effects with 0 errors.

### Automated Tests
- The exact commands and test scripts you will run (e.g. `python {{ scratch_directory }}/test_audit.py`).

### Manual Verification
- Output validation steps, lead schedule reconciliation, and sanity checks.
```

# Walkthrough
